## 6. PreparedStatement — Parameterized Queries

`PreparedStatement` is precompiled and uses `?` placeholders for parameters — **prevents SQL injection** and is faster for repeated queries.

```java
String sql = "SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department = ? AND salary > ?";

try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);
     PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

    // Set parameters (1-indexed, not 0-indexed!)
    pstmt.setString(1, "Engineering");   // first ? = "Engineering"
    pstmt.setDouble(2, 50000.0);         // second ? = 50000.0

    ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();
    while (rs.next()) {
        System.out.println(rs.getString("name") + " - " + rs.getDouble("salary"));
    }

} catch (SQLException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

### Setting Parameters by Type

```java
pstmt.setInt(1, 42);                          // int
pstmt.setLong(1, 123456789L);                 // long
pstmt.setDouble(1, 3.14);                     // double
pstmt.setString(1, "Alice");                  // String
pstmt.setBoolean(1, true);                    // boolean
pstmt.setNull(1, Types.VARCHAR);              // NULL
pstmt.setDate(1, java.sql.Date.valueOf("2024-01-15"));        // date
pstmt.setTimestamp(1, Timestamp.valueOf("2024-01-15 10:30:00")); // timestamp
pstmt.setObject(1, value);                    // generic (JDBC figures out type)
```

### INSERT with PreparedStatement

```java
String sql = "INSERT INTO employees (name, department, salary) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";

try (PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    pstmt.setString(1, "Bob");
    pstmt.setString(2, "Marketing");
    pstmt.setDouble(3, 65000.0);

    int rows = pstmt.executeUpdate();
    System.out.println(rows + " row(s) inserted");
}
```

### Get Auto-Generated Keys (ID)

```java
String sql = "INSERT INTO employees (name, salary) VALUES (?, ?)";

try (PreparedStatement pstmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql, Statement.RETURN_GENERATED_KEYS)) {
    pstmt.setString(1, "Charlie");
    pstmt.setDouble(2, 80000.0);
    pstmt.executeUpdate();

    // Retrieve the generated ID
    ResultSet keys = pstmt.getGeneratedKeys();
    if (keys.next()) {
        long newId = keys.getLong(1);
        System.out.println("New employee ID: " + newId);
    }
}
```

### Statement vs PreparedStatement

| | `Statement` | `PreparedStatement` |
|--|-------------|---------------------|
| SQL injection | Vulnerable | Safe (parameters are escaped) |
| Performance | Compiled every time | Compiled once, reused |
| Readability | String concatenation mess | Clean `?` placeholders |
| Use case | DDL, one-off admin queries | Everything else (all CRUD) |

**Rule: Always use PreparedStatement for any query that includes variables.**

