## 10. Batch Processing

Execute many similar statements efficiently — send them all at once instead of one-by-one:

```java
String sql = "INSERT INTO employees (name, department, salary) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";

try (Connection conn = getConnection();
     PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

    conn.setAutoCommit(false);   // batch in a single transaction

    String[][] data = {
        {"Alice", "IT", "75000"},
        {"Bob", "HR", "65000"},
        {"Charlie", "IT", "80000"},
        {"Diana", "Finance", "70000"}
    };

    for (String[] row : data) {
        ps.setString(1, row[0]);
        ps.setString(2, row[1]);
        ps.setDouble(3, Double.parseDouble(row[2]));
        ps.addBatch();   // add to batch (doesn't execute yet)
    }

    int[] results = ps.executeBatch();   // execute all at once
    conn.commit();

    System.out.println("Inserted " + results.length + " rows");
}
```

**Performance tip:** For large inserts (thousands of rows), batch in chunks of 500–1000:

```java
int batchSize = 500;
int count = 0;
for (String[] row : largeDataset) {
    ps.setString(1, row[0]);
    ps.addBatch();
    if (++count % batchSize == 0) {
        ps.executeBatch();   // flush every 500
    }
}
ps.executeBatch();   // flush remaining
conn.commit();
```

