## 14. Common Errors and Troubleshooting

| Error | Cause | Fix |
|-------|-------|-----|
| `ClassNotFoundException: com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver` | Driver JAR not on classpath | Add MySQL connector JAR to classpath or Maven |
| `No suitable driver found for jdbc:mysql://...` | URL typo or driver not loaded | Check URL format, ensure driver JAR is present |
| `Access denied for user 'root'@'localhost'` | Wrong username/password | Verify credentials, check DB user permissions |
| `Communications link failure` | DB server not running or wrong host/port | Start the DB, check host:port |
| `Connection refused` | Firewall or DB not listening on that port | Check `netstat`, DB config, firewall rules |
| `Too many connections` | Connections not being closed (leaked) | Always use try-with-resources, use connection pool |
| `Column 'X' not found` | Typo in column name in `rs.getString("X")` | Check actual column names in DB schema |
| `Parameter index out of range` | PreparedStatement `?` index wrong (1-based!) | First `?` is `1`, not `0` |
| `ResultSet closed` | Calling `rs.next()` after statement/connection closed | Keep ResultSet inside the try-with-resources scope |

---

## 15. Best Practices

### Resource Management

```java
// ✓ ALWAYS use try-with-resources (auto-closes in reverse order)
try (Connection conn = getConnection();
     PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql);
     ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
    // use rs
}
// Connection, Statement, ResultSet all closed automatically

// ❌ NEVER do this — resource leak
Connection conn = getConnection();
PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql);
ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();
// if exception happens above, nothing gets closed!
```

### Security

```java
// ✓ PreparedStatement — parameters are escaped, SQL injection impossible
ps.setString(1, userInput);

// ❌ String concatenation — SQL injection vulnerable
stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + userInput + "'");
```

### Performance

| Tip | Why |
|-----|-----|
| Use connection pooling (HikariCP) | Avoid TCP handshake per query |
| Use PreparedStatement (not Statement) | Compiled once, executed many times |
| Use batch inserts for bulk data | One network round-trip instead of N |
| Fetch only needed columns | `SELECT name, salary` not `SELECT *` |
| Use indexes in WHERE clauses | Database-side optimization |
| Set fetch size for large results | `ps.setFetchSize(100)` — controls memory |
| Close connections in finally/try-with-resources | Prevent connection leaks |

### Mapping Conventions

```java
// ResultSet → Object (row mapper pattern)
private Employee mapRow(ResultSet rs) throws SQLException {
    return new Employee(
        rs.getLong("id"),
        rs.getString("name"),
        rs.getString("department"),
        rs.getDouble("salary")
    );
}

// Reuse across all SELECT methods — DRY
```

### Summary: JDBC Workflow

```
1. Get connection     → DriverManager.getConnection() or pool.getConnection()
2. Create statement   → conn.prepareStatement(sql)
3. Set parameters     → ps.setString(1, value)
4. Execute            → ps.executeQuery() or ps.executeUpdate()
5. Process results    → while (rs.next()) { rs.getString(...) }
6. Close resources    → try-with-resources handles it
```
